  <h1>Elder Abuse Categories and Common Nursing Home Neglect Patterns</h1>
  <p><em>Reviewed by our editorial team &amp; Long-Term Care Reference. Updated May 2026.</em></p>

  <p>Elder abuse is not a single legal category; it&rsquo;s an umbrella term covering several distinct types of mistreatment. The state Adult Protective Services statutes and the federal nursing home regulations recognize seven recurring categories. Within nursing homes specifically, a smaller set of recurring neglect patterns drives most of the litigation. This page walks through both.</p>

  <h2>The seven recognized categories of elder abuse</h2>

  <h3>1. Physical abuse</h3>

  <p>Intentional use of force resulting in pain or injury. Hitting, slapping, pushing, kicking, force-feeding, inappropriate physical restraints (including the misuse of bed rails as restraints, or the chemical-restraint use of psychotropic medications to subdue rather than to treat). Physical abuse cases in facilities are often documented through unexplained bruising patterns, witness reports from other residents or staff, and (in some cases) facility video footage. F-Tag F600&ndash;F610 deficiencies frequently appear in survey histories.</p>

  <h3>2. Sexual abuse</h3>

  <p>Any non-consensual sexual contact. Especially difficult to detect because many elderly victims (particularly those with cognitive impairment) cannot clearly report. Evidence may come from medical examinations, behavioral changes, sexually transmitted infections, or staff witness reports. The dignity-interest harm in these cases is severe and jury values can be substantial; civil claims often run alongside criminal prosecution.</p>

  <h3>3. Emotional / psychological abuse</h3>

  <p>Verbal assault, threats, humiliation, isolation from family, treating the older adult like an infant. Often the most pervasive category in poorly-run facilities and the hardest to document. Damages are harder to prove than for physical abuse, but pattern evidence (multiple residents, multiple staff witnesses) can build a serious case. Often paired with one or more other categories.</p>

  <h3>4. Neglect</h3>

  <p>The most common form of elder abuse in nursing home settings and the largest source of litigation. Failure to provide food, water, hygiene, medical care, supervision, or a safe environment. Distinguishes between active neglect (intentional withholding) and passive neglect (failure to perform required care, often stemming from understaffing). The federal OBRA regulations and the CMS F-Tag system map directly onto neglect categories; nearly every neglect case has corresponding F-Tag deficiencies in the facility&rsquo;s survey history.</p>

  <h3>5. Financial exploitation</h3>

  <p>Improper use of an elder&rsquo;s funds, property, or assets. Includes outright theft, fraud, scams targeting the elderly, abuse of power of attorney, undue influence over estate-planning instruments, and forced or coerced transfers. Often committed by family members or trusted individuals; sometimes by strangers (lottery scams, romance scams, IRS impersonation, grandparent scams). Statutory enhancements &mdash; treble damages, attorney fees &mdash; are particularly important in this category because the elder&rsquo;s remaining resources may be limited and the wrongdoer&rsquo;s ability to pay is often the binding constraint on recovery.</p>

  <h3>6. Self-neglect</h3>

  <p>The older adult fails to provide for their own basic needs &mdash; often because of cognitive impairment, depression, or inability to manage activities of daily living. Self-neglect is the largest single category in APS caseloads in many states. It is not typically the basis for civil litigation against another party, but where a caregiver or facility was on notice and failed to intervene, the analysis can shift to a neglect claim against that party.</p>

  <h3>7. Abandonment</h3>

  <p>Desertion by a caregiver who had assumed responsibility for providing care. Includes leaving a dependent adult unattended at a hospital, in a public place, or in their home without arrangements for ongoing care. Often overlaps with neglect.</p>

  <h2>Common nursing home neglect patterns</h2>

  <p>Within the broader neglect category, a relatively small set of recurring patterns dominates nursing home litigation. Each has its own clinical signature, its own F-Tag, and its own valuation profile.</p>

  <h3>Pressure ulcers (bedsores)</h3>

  <p>The single most common claim in nursing home neglect litigation. Pressure ulcers are staged I through IV by the National Pressure Injury Advisory Panel system:</p>

  <ul>
    <li><strong>Stage I:</strong> intact skin with non-blanchable redness. Reversible with proper care.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage II:</strong> partial-thickness skin loss with exposed dermis. Painful but typically healable.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage III:</strong> full-thickness skin loss extending into subcutaneous tissue. Often actionable.</li>
    <li><strong>Stage IV:</strong> full-thickness loss with exposed bone, tendon, or muscle. Almost always actionable and a frequent precipitant of sepsis and death.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>The standard of care requires regular skin assessment, repositioning every two hours for bed-bound residents, pressure-relieving surfaces, attention to nutrition and hydration (both of which affect skin integrity), and prompt response when early-stage ulceration is identified. F-Tag F686 governs this area. A facility-acquired Stage III or IV pressure ulcer is generally treated as presumptively the result of inadequate care; the facility&rsquo;s defense typically focuses on pre-existing condition or rapid decline despite proper care.</p>

  <h3>Falls (broken hips and head injuries)</h3>

  <p>The second most common claim. Hip fractures in elderly patients carry approximately 20&ndash;30% one-year mortality and frequently trigger a chain of declining function. Head injuries from falls produce subdural hematomas, traumatic brain injury, and (especially in patients on anticoagulants) catastrophic outcomes. Falls cases turn on the facility&rsquo;s fall-risk assessment, the care plan it developed, and whether the planned interventions (bed alarms, low beds, supervision, gait belts, scheduled toileting) were actually implemented. F-Tag F689 governs accident-hazard and supervision deficiencies.</p>

  <h3>Malnutrition and dehydration</h3>

  <p>Malnutrition can develop in a vulnerable resident within weeks of inadequate feeding assistance. Dehydration is faster &mdash; days. Both are associated with pressure ulcer development, infection vulnerability, and falls. F-Tag F692 governs this area. Documentation in these cases often comes from weight records (a 5%-in-30-days weight loss is a recognized clinical threshold), intake and output records, laboratory values (elevated BUN, sodium), and the medical complications that follow (urinary tract infection, pneumonia, pressure ulcers).</p>

  <h3>Medication errors</h3>

  <p>Wrong drug, wrong dose, wrong patient, missed dose, drug interactions, and inappropriate use of psychotropic medications (chemical restraints). F-Tags F757&ndash;F761 govern this area. Medication errors that produce immediate harm (e.g., a missed insulin dose leading to diabetic ketoacidosis) are easier to litigate than long-running patterns of inappropriate psychotropic use, but the latter can support substantial recoveries with the right expert work.</p>

  <h3>Wandering and elopement</h3>

  <p>Residents with cognitive impairment may wander within or off facility property. Elopement (leaving the facility unsupervised) can be catastrophic in cold weather, in traffic-heavy areas, or near water hazards. F-Tag F689 again governs. These cases typically turn on the facility&rsquo;s elopement-risk assessment and the adequacy of its security and supervision systems (door alarms, wander-management systems, staffing levels).</p>

  <h3>Infection control and sepsis</h3>

  <p>Untreated pressure ulcers and untreated urinary tract infections are the leading paths to sepsis in nursing home residents. Sepsis carries high mortality even with prompt treatment. The litigation case typically traces the chain: deficient routine care produced the underlying infection or wound, deficient assessment failed to identify the early signs, and a delay in hospital transfer worsened the outcome.</p>

  <h2>How abuse type affects compensation</h2>

  <p>The abuse type and resulting harm drive the medical and pain-and-suffering damages. Documentary record strength &mdash; facility charts, CMS surveys, prior F-Tag deficiencies, APS findings, ombudsman complaints &mdash; drives liability strength. The state framework (CA EADACPA, Illinois NHCA, Florida Ch. 415, etc.) drives the availability of attorney fees, treble damages, and pre-death pain-and-suffering recovery. The defendant&rsquo;s insurance and assets ultimately constrain practical recovery.</p>

